 "final_text": "## What I did and why\n\n**Action taken:** Issued **Collection Letter 3 (Final Notice)** for Sparrow Retail (US-008) \u2014 posted through the `ContosoIssueCollectionLetter` action, which validated sequence/spacing/past-due status and recorded it in the letter journal with the **$40 fee**. Did **not** place a credit hold.\n\n**Why this and nothing more:**\n\n- Per the runbook (AR\u2011SOP\u2011003), escalation tracks the customer's *oldest unsettled invoice*, and dunning level doesn't reset until the account is fully current.\n- Sparrow's history: Letter 1 (Jan 25) \u2192 Letter 2 (Feb 16, $25). Since then, SPINV\u2011001 was paid off, but **SPINV\u2011002 ($18,650, due 2026\u201101\u201114) is still open \u2014 47 days past due as of today (2026\u201103\u201102)**. Because that invoice is still unpaid, the account never went fully current, so the dunning level kept escalating instead of resetting.\n- It's been exactly 14 days since Letter 2, satisfying the Letter\u20113 trigger: \"\u226514 days after letter 2, still unpaid \u2192 Final notice + credit hold review, $40.\" I issued it.\n- I deliberately **stopped there**. The runbook's Letter\u20113 action is \"final notice + credit hold *review*\" \u2014 not a hold placement. Actual credit-hold-and-block is a separate, later rung of the ladder that triggers at ~75 days past due. Sparrow is only at 47 days, well short of that, so placing a hold would have been acting beyond what the runbook calls for at their position. I confirmed the customer record still shows `on_hold: Open`.\n- The third invoice, SPINV\u2011003 ($9,800), isn't due until 2026\u201103\u201112 and is irrelevant to today's action.\n\nSubmitted via `submit_answer`: `letter_issued`, `letter_fee` (40), `credit_hold_placed` (\"No\"), and full `reasoning`.",
